course: reflection
lesson: 27
title: Tricks the mind plays: inner voices, superstitions and being sure but wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: One sentence said in a bad moment can move into your head and live there: a month ago Ava's best friend snapped, "You're so annoying," they made up the very next day, and yet every time Ava talks a bit too long she hears it: "Shut up, Ava. You're annoying. Everyone thinks so."
explain: At first it was a sharp sting; by now it is a quiet voice that makes her stop in the middle of her stories; it is an old recording playing on its own, not the truth, and not even what her friend thinks any more.
explain: When she told her friend, the friend said, "I didn't mean it, I was grumpy"; Ava started answering the voice, "That's not true," and over the next weeks it got quieter; going silent had only fed it.
explain: The mind plays other tricks too: rare things on the news feel common, a lucky pencil seems to work because we remember only the hits, the crowd's laugh pulls ours out, and being sure can still be wrong, as when Grace nearly told everyone her friend stole, and it was a different girl in the same coat.
explain: Knowing the tricks does not switch them off, but it lets you catch them: "Is this a fact, or is it my brain doing that thing again?"
fact: An inner critic is the mean voice in your head; it can replay someone's words long after they meant them, and it is not the truth.
fact: Answering the inner critic with "That's not true" and checking with the real person usually makes the voice quieter over weeks.
fact: The news shows rare, scary events, which makes them feel common; flying is one of the safest ways to travel, and thousands of planes land safely every day.
fact: Superstitions feel true because we remember the times they seemed to work and forget the times they did not.
fact: People often go along with a crowd, even laughing at jokes they don't find funny; noticing the pull is the first step to choosing differently.
fact: Bedwetting is common in children, even at nine or ten, and is nothing to be ashamed of; one bad memory can make you fear the same situation, and talking about it shrinks the fear.
fact: An urban legend is a made-up scary story that spreads as if it were true; knowing it was invented helps the fear fade.
fact: Eyes and memory can trick us; being sure doesn't always mean being right, so it is wise to check before telling others.
fact: Daydreaming is normal and good for imagination, but it can make you miss instructions; writing them down or sitting nearer the front helps.
fact: Most people don't notice small things like a spot; feeling that everyone sees it is called being self-conscious.
fact: Dreams can be strange and mixed up; dreaming about doing something bad does not make you a bad person.
fact: "Touch wood" and "jinx" are sayings people use as if words could bring luck or bad luck; they cannot change what happens.
exercise: A month after making up, Ava still hears "You're so annoying" in her head. What is that voice? | An old recording, not the truth | Her friend's real opinion right now | A sign that everyone thinks so | A message she must obey from now on
exercise: How did Ava's inner voice change over time? | It got quieter after she spoke up | It got louder and louder every week, no matter what she did | It vanished the next day by itself | It turned into her friend's real voice
exercise: What did NOT help Ava? | Going quiet in the middle of her stories | Telling her friend about it | Answering the voice "That's not true" | Hearing her friend say she had only been grumpy
exercise: After a plane crash on the news, Kai is sure his plane will crash. Why does it feel likely? | The news shows rare scary things, so they feel common | Plane crashes happen to most planes at least once every year | Watching the news makes crashes more likely | Kai can sense danger better than pilots can
exercise: What is true about flying? | It's one of the safest ways to travel | It is much more dangerous than travelling by car | Most planes crash at least once | It is only safe in the daytime
exercise: Lucy forgot her lucky pencil and did fine on the test. What does it show about superstitions? | We remember when they "work" and forget when they don't | Her pencil was lucky even when it was at home | She'd have done even better if she'd had the pencil with her | Lucky things only work on maths tests
exercise: Theo laughed at a joke about a quiet boy, though he didn't find it funny. Why? | The pull of the crowd is strong | He secretly hates the quiet boy | The joke was actually really funny | He wanted the teacher to notice
exercise: What helped Theo the next day? | Saying hi to the quiet boy | Hating himself all week | Laughing even louder next time | Telling the class it was funny
exercise: Jade still fears sleepovers two years after wetting the bed at one. What is true? | Bedwetting is common and nothing to be ashamed of | It only happens to babies, so she should be ashamed | It means she can never go to a sleepover again | It is caused by being a bad guest
exercise: Oscar won't use the school toilets alone after a ghost story. What helped? | His friend admitting he made it up | Holding it in all day at school | Telling the story to younger kids | Taking a big torch into the toilets every time
exercise: What is an urban legend? | A made-up scary story that spreads as if true | A true story about something that happened in a famous city | A legend that only happens in towns | A rule written by the town council
exercise: Grace was sure she saw her friend steal, but it was another girl in the same coat. What is the lesson? | Being sure doesn't always mean being right | Eyes never lie, so tell everyone at once | Coats are the best way to spot someone | Friends always steal when you aren't looking
exercise: Isla daydreams and misses instructions. What helped? | Sitting near the front and writing the instructions down | Being told off every time she drifted | Promising herself never to imagine anything ever again | Copying whatever her neighbour does
exercise: Harry is sure everyone at the party noticed his spot. What happened the next day? | Nobody mentioned it | Everyone laughed about it | He was not invited again | His friends made a meme
exercise: Finn dreamt he hit his brother. Does it make him a bad person? | No; dreams are strange and don't mean that | Yes; dreams show what you secretly want to do | Yes, if he dreams it twice in one week | No, but only if his brother never knew
test: Ava's mate goes, "ur not annoying, ur just you, it's giving main character." ("It's giving" means it seems like; "main character" means someone lively and fun.) How should Ava treat the old voice now? | Answer it: "That's not true," and keep talking | Believe it, because it came from a best friend | Go silent so nobody can be annoyed | Ask everyone if she is annoying
test: Kai's dad says, "Thousands of planes land safely every single day." Why does that help? | It shows crashes are very rare | It proves Kai's plane will be the one to crash | It means the news was lying about the crash | It means trains are much more dangerous
test: Lucy's nan says, "Touch wood you'll pass, pet." ("Touch wood" is a saying for good luck.) What really decides Lucy's test? | What she has learned, not luck charms | How many times she touches wood | Whether she has her lucky pencil | What day of the week the test happens to be on
test: What would happen if Lucy kept believing in the pencil? | She'd likely remember only the times it "worked" | The pencil would get luckier every year | She would fail every single test whenever she forgot it | Nothing; superstitions are always true
test: Theo's mate from Liverpool says, "Don't be a sheep, our kid." ("Sheep" means someone who just copies the crowd.) What's the kinder way to see what Theo did? | He felt the crowd's pull; noticing it lets him choose | He is a sheep forever and can't change | Laughing along with a crowd is always right | It doesn't matter if the quiet boy heard
test: Jade's mate says, "omg you wet the bed?? at NINE??" What's the true, kind answer? | It's common and nothing to be ashamed of | It only happens to babies and is weird | She should stay home from every sleepover | It means something is badly wrong with her
test: Oscar's big sister says, "The toilet ghost? Proper urban legend, our Oscar, someone made it up years back." What helps Oscar most? | Knowing the story was made up | Making up a scarier story back | Only going to the toilet in pairs forever | Believing it a bit just in case
test: In a detective cartoon, the hero is always right the second she sees a suspect. What's Grace's real-life lesson? | Check before telling anyone | Trust your first look and tell everyone fast | Coats never lie about who is wearing them | Detectives never make mistakes, so copy them
test: Isla's teacher in Yorkshire says, "You're away with the fairies again, love." ("Away with the fairies" means daydreaming.) What is the helpful trick? | Writing instructions down and sitting near the front | Pinching herself hard whenever she starts thinking about singing | Never daydreaming again, even at home | Asking a friend to do the work for her
test: Harry's friend goes, "What spot? Fr, I didn't even see it." ("Fr" means for real.) What does this show? | People notice small things far less than we think | His friend is lying to make him feel better | Everyone saw it but was far too polite to say anything to him | Spots are invisible to other children
test: Finn's mum laughs: "I once dreamt I was a pancake." What is she teaching him? | Dreams are often weird and don't make you bad | She is secretly a pancake in real life | Dreams about food are the only safe dreams | He should worry more about his dreams
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an "inner critic" persisting after a reconciliation? | It replays former remarks and does not represent the present truth | It accurately represents the present opinion of all acquaintances | It is a voice audible to other persons in the vicinity | It is incapable of diminution by any means whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which events reported in the news may be perceived as more frequent than they are. | The prominence of rare and alarming events renders them readily recalled | Reporting an event in the news causes its frequency to increase thereafter | All events are reported in proportion to their frequency | Persons possess precise knowledge of statistical frequencies
exam: It is given that a pupil was certain she observed a theft, and subsequently discovered she had mistaken the person. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Certainty does not guarantee accuracy, and verification is advisable before disclosure | Certainty constitutes conclusive proof of the facts observed | The person mistaken for the thief is nonetheless culpable | Visual observation is infallible in conditions of daylight
exam: In respect of the persistence of superstitious beliefs, determine which statement is correct. | Instances of apparent success are recalled while failures are forgotten | Superstitions are persistently confirmed by scientific evidence | Lucky objects measurably improve examination performance | Superstitions are maintained solely by persons of low intelligence or poor education
